|  Digital Research C READ.ME File Notes	        May 1984 |
|								 |
|								 |
|	 1. The  following   rules   apply  to  any  assembly    |
|           language  routine  designed to  interface  with a	 |
|           C program.				                 |
|								 |
|	  o If the assembly program  alters  values in the ES    |
|	    register,  you must save the original value in ES    |
|           upon entry to the routine.   You must restore the    |
|	    original ES value upon exit from the routine.  If    |
|	    you call a C function from  the assembly routine,    |
|	    you must restore the original ES before the call.    |
|	    This applies for small model C programs only.        |
|								 |
|         o If your program sets the direction flag, you must    |
|           clear it upon exit from the routine.                 |
|								 |
|	 2. The C compiler now  has  two  additional  command  	 |
|	    line options.					 |
|	    							 |
|         o Use the -E option to change the maximum number of	 |
|	    errors the compiler detects before  stopping  the	 |
|	    compilation.        The  default  is  10  errors.	 |
|	    The following example changes the  maximum number	 |
|	    of errors to 25.					 |
|								 |
|		B>DRC PROGRAM -E25				 |
|								 |
|	  o Use the -S option  to  reduce  the  size  of  the	 |
|	    symbol table by truncating  symbolic names during	 |
|	    compilation.  Specify a  length  after  the -S as	 |
|	    shown in the following example.  In this example,	 |
|	    all symbolic names are truncated to 8 characters.	 |
|								 |
|		B>DRC PROGRAM -S8				 |
|								 |
|	 3. Symbolic names  are now significant up  to  forty	 |
|	    (40) characters instead of eight (8).  Use the -S	 |
|	    option to  reduce  the  symbol  table  size  when	 |
|	    compiling large programs that use lengthy  names.	 |
|								 |
|	 4. The compiler displays  a  warning message if your	 |
|	    program attempts to compute the address (&)  of a	 |
|	    register variable.				 	 |
|								 |
|				Page 1				 |
|								 |
------------------------------------------------------------------















------------------------------------------------------------------
|  Digital Research C READ.ME File Notes	        May 1984 |
|								 |
|								 |
|								 |
|	 5. You can create overlays  that  either  accumulate	 |
|	    or overlay the data in various overlays.  Use the	 |
|	    LINK86 CUM  option  to  specify cumulative  data.	 |
|	    Use  the   LINK86   NOCUM   option   to   specify	 |
|	    non-cumulative data.  Overlays on the same  level	 |
|	    share data areas.					 |
|								 |
|	 6. The  C  compiler  has  three  additional  library	 |
|	    functions: readl, writel, and rename.		 |
|								 |
|	  o The readl function is just like the read function	 |
|	    except readl uses a long int argument and returns	 |
|	    a long int value.					 |
|								 |
|	  o The  writel  function  is  just  like  the  write	 |
|	    function except that it uses a long int  argument	 |
|	    and returns a long int value.			 |
|								 |
|	  o Use the rename function to change  the name  of a	 |
|	    file.  This function uses two parameters as shown	 |
|	    in the following syntax specification.		 |
|								 |
|		rename(oldname,newname)				 |
|								 |
|	    The rename function returns a 1 if successful and	 |
|	    a 0 if the file has a Read/Only  status  or  does	 |
|	    not exist.						 |
|								 |
|	 7. If you mix a C  program  with  assembly  language	 |
|	    routines, your main program must be a  C function	 |
|	    to  initialize  the  C  environment.   This  only	 |
|	    requires a few extra bytes of space as  shown  in	 |
|	    the following example.				 |
|								 |
|		main(){maina86();}				 |
|								 |
|	 8. The -X option  has been disabled because the size	 |
|	    of  the  inline  code  that  saves  and  restores	 |
|	    registers in an assembly routine call is smaller.	 |
|								 |
|	 9. The  -c and -m  options  for  compact  and medium	 |
|	    model compilation are no longer supported.		 |
|								 |
|				Page 2				 |
|								 |
